1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,140
How do we calculate a point distance from a
floor and its location within a triangle?

2
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:08,040
We will observe game platforms and terrain,

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:10,880
then calculate plane normals and signed
distances.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:12,960
Next we will build a triangle from three
points and

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:16,280
work out its area and barycentric coordinates.

6
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:21,980
Finally we will reject an off-plane query and
use the same coordinates to interpolate color.

7
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:26,610
We travel along paths and bridges on a Serious
Sam2 shoreline.

8
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:29,110
Watch the floor and bridge top surfaces.

9
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:34,410
A reference lets us distinguish floating above
that surface from approaching it.

10
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:37,710
Mathematics can simplify that reference to a
plane.

11
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:41,710
A real platform has thickness and edges; a
plane extends infinitely.

12
00:00:41,790 --> 00:00:46,030
Distance to the surface and remaining within

13
00:00:46,310 --> 00:00:48,690
the platform footprint are separate tests.

14
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:51,790
Camera motion changes its screen appearance.

15
00:00:51,790 --> 00:00:55,170
Calculations must use points and normals
defined in one coordinate frame.

16
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,290
This footage motivates a reference surface;

17
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:02,290
it does not reveal the game internal collision
representation.

18
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:07,040
Describe a plane with a normal and a location
coefficient.

19
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:10,680
The normal points perpendicular to the
surface.

20
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,860
Every point on the plane has the same dot
product

21
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:17,300
with its normal. Call that coefficient d.

22
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:22,820
The normal has components zero, one, zero.
Constant d equals two.

23
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:25,920
Every point with y equal to two lies on this
plane.

24
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:31,400
The y zero plane contains the origin; the y
two plane does not.

25
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:35,720
A general plane is an affine set, not
necessarily a linear subspace.

26
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:39,260
Subtracting two plane points gives a direction
along the plane.

27
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:41,660
Its dot product with the normal is zero.

28
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:48,080
Reversing the normal swaps front and back.
Reverse d as well to preserve the same plane.

29
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:53,750
Evaluate the normal dot point minus d. A
positive value lies on the normal side;

30
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:56,970
a negative value lies on the opposite side.

31
00:01:57,210 --> 00:02:02,110
The normal has components zero, two, zero.
Constant d equals four.

32
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:05,690
This is still the y-equals-two plane, and the
normal length is two.

33
00:02:06,010 --> 00:02:09,250
At point four, five, minus three, the dot
product is ten.

34
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:12,470
Subtracting four gives six, but six is not the
distance.

35
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:15,950
Divide by normal length two to get signed
distance three.

36
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:18,970
Only a unit normal makes the raw residual a
distance.

37
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:23,950
Normalize both the normal and d by two.

38
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:26,590
Normalizing only the normal changes the plane
location.

39
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:32,730
A zero-length normal cannot define a plane
direction. Validate before division.

40
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:37,360
In Noita, the character moves through layered
terrain.

41
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:40,460
Use the waterline and wooden floor as visible
references.

42
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:46,340
The sign of a height difference distinguishes
positions above or below the same reference.

43
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:51,200
Following camera motion does not prevent
relative comparison.

44
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:55,160
The screen bottom is not a fixed world floor.

45
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:59,980
For a horizontal reference, subtract its
height from y.

46
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:04,380
A tilted surface instead needs distance along
its normal.

47
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:09,640
Replacing layered floors with one infinite
plane loses holes and edges.

48
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:12,380
Surface distance and boundary checks are
separate.

49
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,940
Our next diagram calculates the closest point
on an explicitly defined plane;

50
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,580
it does not claim to reproduce the pixel
terrain implementation.

51
00:03:21,050 --> 00:03:27,190
Point four, five, minus three has signed
distance three from the y two plane.

52
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:33,450
Move three units opposite its normal. The
closest plane point is four, two, minus three.

53
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:38,010
With a unit normal, subtract signed distance
times that normal from the query.

54
00:03:38,290 --> 00:03:41,490
With an unnormalized normal,

55
00:03:41,490 --> 00:03:45,030
multiply it by the residual divided by its
squared length, then subtract.

56
00:03:45,290 --> 00:03:48,210
Behind the plane, negative signed distance
moves the point toward the normal.

57
00:03:48,210 --> 00:03:51,390
One formula handles both sides.

58
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:56,870
This projection finds the nearest point on an
infinite plane. Beyond a triangle

59
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:00,630
or platform edge, the nearest point on that
bounded surface needs a separate test.

60
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:04,800
Three noncollinear points determine a plane.

61
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:09,060
Form two edge vectors from the same vertex.

62
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:15,340
Edges six, zero, zero and zero, four, zero
have cross product zero, zero, twenty-four.

63
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:19,800
Using our right-handed first-edge cross
second-edge

64
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:21,940
convention gives unit normal zero, zero, one.

65
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:25,500
Swapping the edge order reverses the normal.

66
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:27,940
Define winding and front-face conventions
together.

67
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:31,080
Dot any one vertex with the normal to obtain
d.

68
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:34,360
All three z coordinates are zero here, so d is
zero.

69
00:04:34,528 --> 00:04:36,900
Collinear points yield a zero cross product.

70
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:41,760
Nearly collinear inputs may also be poorly
conditioned; check before normalization.

71
00:04:42,430 --> 00:04:47,010
Watch a bright triangular rock marking and the
grapple attached nearby.

72
00:04:47,113 --> 00:04:50,770
Separate its three vertices from locations
within it.

73
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:55,050
Camera motion tilts and compresses its screen
appearance.

74
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:58,370
Distinguish a surface shape from its
projection.

75
00:04:58,610 --> 00:05:02,970
Ordering three points around the boundary
defines a front-face direction.

76
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:06,690
Reversing that order reverses the chosen
normal.

77
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:12,090
A triangular marking does not reveal the
entire rock mesh.

78
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:15,410
We connect visible features to an explicitly
defined mathematical example.

79
00:05:15,530 --> 00:05:19,610
Observe which reference vertex lies closest to
the attachment.

80
00:05:20,130 --> 00:05:23,850
We will express relative locations using three
weights.

81
00:05:24,230 --> 00:05:26,550
First we will handle more than three ordered
vertices,

82
00:05:26,550 --> 00:05:29,310
then calculate triangle lengths and area.

83
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:35,780
Choosing any three polygon vertices can be
unstable

84
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:40,380
when they are nearly collinear or lie at a
concave corner.

85
00:05:40,513 --> 00:05:45,000
Newell method accumulates contributions from
adjacent

86
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:47,860
vertices in boundary order, including the
closing edge.

87
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:52,660
Its z component sums each neighboring x
difference times their y sum.

88
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:55,580
Cycle the coordinates for the other
components.

89
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:58,840
Reversing boundary order reverses the normal.

90
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:02,240
Arbitrarily shuffled points are not valid
ordered input.

91
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:07,300
Once the normal is chosen, average its dot
products with vertices to obtain d,

92
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:10,460
equivalently dot the mean point with the
normal.

93
00:06:10,620 --> 00:06:15,420
The source calls this best fit, but it is not
generally the least-squares plane

94
00:06:15,420 --> 00:06:19,980
of an unordered point cloud. Validate point
count and degeneracy separately.

95
00:06:20,450 --> 00:06:24,930
Give each edge the same index as its opposite
vertex.

96
00:06:25,170 --> 00:06:27,230
Adding the three edge lengths gives the
perimeter.

97
00:06:27,430 --> 00:06:32,790
A three-four-five right triangle has perimeter
twelve and semiperimeter six.

98
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:36,390
Edge length divided by the sine of its
opposite

99
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:39,770
angle is the same at all three vertices: the
sine law.

100
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:44,230
Given two edges and their included angle,

101
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:46,830
the cosine law finds the remaining edge using
squared lengths.

102
00:06:47,030 --> 00:06:49,930
Here three squared plus four squared equals
five squared.

103
00:06:49,930 --> 00:06:52,510
The right angle makes the cosine term zero.

104
00:06:52,890 --> 00:06:55,550
Do not mix degrees and radians.

105
00:06:55,750 --> 00:06:59,890
Validate positive lengths and triangle
feasibility after solving.

106
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:04,100
Observe platform ends and rock corners in
Noita.

107
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:07,240
We can choose three visible locations as
reference points.

108
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:12,020
Movement from one reference toward another
changes which point the character is nearer.

109
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:14,180
Cartesian coordinates describe the location;

110
00:07:14,363 --> 00:07:18,180
three weights describe it relative to a
triangle that moves with its references.

111
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:21,460
Track all three references when the camera
moves.

112
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:25,700
Do not combine pixels from incompatible screen
frames.

113
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:30,660
Choosing three points does not make the entire

114
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:32,880
ground a triangle; gaps may lie between them.

115
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:36,960
Our next example defines its own triangle,
calculates area,

116
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:39,660
and expresses inside and outside positions
with three numbers.

117
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:45,350
Use vertices zero, zero; four, zero; and zero,
three.

118
00:07:45,530 --> 00:07:49,150
Base four times height three divided by two
gives area six.

119
00:07:49,350 --> 00:07:53,350
With side lengths three, four and five,

120
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:57,050
Heron formula uses semiperimeter six and its
differences from each edge.

121
00:07:57,350 --> 00:08:00,010
The radicand is six times three times two
times one,

122
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:02,730
or thirty-six. Its square root is six.

123
00:08:03,050 --> 00:08:08,810
The cross-product length is the parallelogram
area. Half gives the triangle area.

124
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:14,650
Here the parallelogram area is twelve, and
dividing by two gives a triangle area of six.

125
00:08:14,756 --> 00:08:17,870
In2D, the corresponding determinant has a
sign.

126
00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:21,970
Reversing winding changes the sign but not
area magnitude.

127
00:08:22,086 --> 00:08:25,410
Taking a3D cross-product magnitude discards
direction.

128
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:28,690
Distinguish area from front-face orientation.

129
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:35,120
Barycentric coordinates give weights for three
vertices. Their sum must be one.

130
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:38,660
Multiply each vertex by its weight and add.

131
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:41,440
The first vertex has coordinates one, zero,
zero.

132
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:45,700
The midpoint of the first two vertices is one
half, one half,

133
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:48,980
zero. Equal one-third weights give the vertex
average.

134
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:52,940
Although written as three numbers, only two
are independent because their sum is fixed.

135
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,520
A triangle surface is two-dimensional.

136
00:08:55,520 --> 00:09:00,100
Using the third vertex as origin gives axes
first-minus-third and second-minus-third.

137
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:02,920
They need not be orthogonal or unit length.

138
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:07,700
On a nondegenerate triangle plane, nonnegative
weights describe its interior and boundary.

139
00:09:08,040 --> 00:09:11,320
Negative weights meaningfully describe outside
points.

140
00:09:11,930 --> 00:09:15,170
Connecting to another rock changes the marking
screen position.

141
00:09:15,250 --> 00:09:18,550
Choose three reference points around the
attachment marking.

142
00:09:18,690 --> 00:09:22,350
Weights relative to the same three vertices
provide a

143
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:25,570
location reference even as a surface or camera
moves.

144
00:09:25,770 --> 00:09:30,890
Approaching a vertex makes its weight approach
the number one.

145
00:09:31,450 --> 00:09:34,870
A point on its opposite edge has zero weight
for it.

146
00:09:35,210 --> 00:09:40,670
Screen proximity is not automatically the3D
barycentric

147
00:09:40,670 --> 00:09:43,170
relationship because perspective changes the
projection.

148
00:09:43,890 --> 00:09:47,810
We have not extracted the real attachment
coordinates.

149
00:09:48,110 --> 00:09:51,990
Visible markings motivate the explicitly
defined numerical example.

150
00:09:52,530 --> 00:09:55,070
Now we will multiply vertices by weights and
see

151
00:09:55,330 --> 00:09:59,670
how a negative weight places a point outside.

152
00:10:00,327 --> 00:10:04,560
Define3D vertices zero, zero, zero; six, zero,

153
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:08,360
zero; and zero, four, zero. All lie in the z
zero plane.

154
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:12,300
Weights point two, point three and point five
sum to one.

155
00:10:12,540 --> 00:10:16,540
Compute x as six times point three and y as
four times point five.

156
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:22,280
The result is one point eight, two, zero. All
weights are nonnegative, so it lies inside.

157
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:26,460
Now use minus point two, point seven and point
five.

158
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:29,620
Their sum is still one, but the first weight
is negative.

159
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:36,420
The result four point two, two, zero lies
beyond the edge opposite the first vertex.

160
00:10:36,580 --> 00:10:40,120
Position has length units; weights are
dimensionless ratios. Rejecting every

161
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:45,840
negative weight discards information
describing valid outside positions.

162
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:49,670
Given a position, obtain each weight from a
signed

163
00:10:49,870 --> 00:10:54,210
subtriangle area divided by the whole signed
area.

164
00:10:54,470 --> 00:11:02,230
For the inside example, the subareas are
twenty, thirty and fifty percent of the total.

165
00:11:02,610 --> 00:11:06,770
Keep consistent vertex order and signed areas.

166
00:11:07,190 --> 00:11:09,570
Absolute areas discard the negative weight of
an outside point.

167
00:11:09,870 --> 00:11:12,970
Crossing the edge opposite the first vertex
reverses

168
00:11:12,970 --> 00:11:15,710
that subarea sign and makes its weight
negative.

169
00:11:15,710 --> 00:11:19,770
Reversing all winding reverses numerator and
denominator together,

170
00:11:19,770 --> 00:11:22,870
preserving weights. Be consistent.

171
00:11:23,130 --> 00:11:25,590
A zero whole area cannot be divided by.

172
00:11:25,770 --> 00:11:30,270
Very thin triangles may amplify small
coordinate errors.

173
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:36,100
The character moves among ledges at different
heights.

174
00:11:36,300 --> 00:11:39,160
Distinguish visual overlap from occupying the
same surface.

175
00:11:39,316 --> 00:11:42,480
This2D footage exposes two position
components; in3D,

176
00:11:42,527 --> 00:11:46,580
different depths may project to the same
screen location.

177
00:11:46,920 --> 00:11:49,580
An apparently inside projected location alone
does

178
00:11:49,580 --> 00:11:52,060
not prove that a3D query lies on a triangle
surface.

179
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:58,000
Separate distance-to-plane validation from
within-plane barycentric tests.

180
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,840
Passing a platform edge can also leave its
finite footprint while

181
00:12:03,300 --> 00:12:06,000
keeping the same reference height. Remember
negative outside weights.

182
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:11,980
Our diagram will compare two points with
identical projection and different depths.

183
00:12:12,450 --> 00:12:19,650
Our inside point was one point eight, two,
zero. Keep x and y but change z to seven.

184
00:12:19,890 --> 00:12:22,930
Both points have the same XY projection,

185
00:12:23,230 --> 00:12:26,990
but the new point is seven units off the
triangle plane.

186
00:12:27,250 --> 00:12:31,130
For a projected solve, dropping the axis with
largest absolute

187
00:12:31,130 --> 00:12:34,630
normal component helps choose a
well-conditioned projection.

188
00:12:34,870 --> 00:12:37,630
The discarded coordinate still matters.

189
00:12:37,870 --> 00:12:42,430
Check plane distance separately before testing
projected weights.

190
00:12:42,450 --> 00:12:47,450
Plane proximity tolerance has spatial units
and scale;

191
00:12:47,810 --> 00:12:50,890
barycentric tolerance uses dimensionless
ratios.

192
00:12:51,190 --> 00:12:55,270
Also validate finite inputs, normal length and
the area denominator.

193
00:12:55,490 --> 00:12:58,710
An inside test is not complete input
validation.

194
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:03,040
We move among narrow ledges and slanted
terrain in Noita.

195
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:06,060
Track three nearby corners together.

196
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:11,620
A thin reference triangle can turn a small
position change into a large weight change.

197
00:13:11,880 --> 00:13:16,700
Producing three numbers does not guarantee
numerical

198
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:19,360
stability for nearly zero area. Inspect the
input shape as well.

199
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:24,540
Changing reference vertices changes the
position represented by the same weights.

200
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:27,000
Coordinates and their triangle form one
description.

201
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:31,120
Real boundaries contain notches and gaps;

202
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:35,680
filling a mathematical triangle between
references does not reproduce the terrain.

203
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:39,100
Today concerns one plane and triangle.

204
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:43,360
The next lecture partitions more complex
boundaries into triangles.

205
00:13:43,830 --> 00:13:46,710
Barycentric coordinates combine attributes as
well as positions.

206
00:13:46,798 --> 00:13:51,190
Assign different values to the three vertices.

207
00:13:51,430 --> 00:13:57,610
Give the vertices red, green and blue in an
explicitly linear color example.

208
00:13:57,890 --> 00:14:04,750
Weights point two, point three and point five
produce those same three color components.

209
00:14:05,270 --> 00:14:11,850
An edge midpoint averages its endpoints; a
vertex reproduces its own attribute.

210
00:14:11,850 --> 00:14:17,670
The same construction interpolates defined
heights or texture coordinates.

211
00:14:18,350 --> 00:14:21,330
Direction attributes that require
normalization need a further check.

212
00:14:21,670 --> 00:14:24,090
This is affine interpolation on a triangle
surface.

213
00:14:24,630 --> 00:14:29,330
Perspective-correct interpolation in screen
space belongs to the rendering chapter.

214
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:33,300
Observe differently colored roofs and walls in
Serious Sam2.

215
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:38,160
Appearance varies with surface location.

216
00:14:38,480 --> 00:14:41,380
Finding a surface position and then finding
its

217
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:44,240
attributes are two connected graphics
questions.

218
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:49,440
If values are stored per triangle vertex, our
weights construct intermediate values.

219
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:51,820
This is the mathematical model we explicitly
defined.

220
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:56,540
The real image may involve textures, lighting
and effects.

221
00:14:56,540 --> 00:15:00,620
Footage alone does not confirm vertex-color
interpolation.

222
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:04,460
Preserve surface references as the camera
moves.

223
00:15:04,980 --> 00:15:08,340
Do not mix screen coordinates with
object-surface coordinates.

224
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:12,940
Our final problems validate plane distance and
triangle weights

225
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:16,780
together: locate the point, then calculate its
attributes.

226
00:15:17,370 --> 00:15:22,910
First problem: the normal has components zero,
two, zero, and constant d equals four.

227
00:15:23,090 --> 00:15:26,710
What is the distance from this plane to point
four, five, minus three?

228
00:15:26,970 --> 00:15:31,230
Residual six divided by normal length two
gives distance three.

229
00:15:31,330 --> 00:15:34,590
The closest plane point is four, two, minus
three.

230
00:15:34,850 --> 00:15:38,730
Second: on our z zero triangle, weights point
